  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1: The coastal flood risk will linger into early next \nweek.\n\nAs a nor'easter churns offshore of the Eastern Seaboard, steady west-\nnorthwesterly flow will continue across the Lowcountry and southeast \nGeorgia. Even though this will create a less favorable environment \nfor coastal flooding, the astro tide will still be elevated given \nthe recent full moon. Thus, still expect to see minor (and possibly \nmoderate) coastal flooding with each high tide cycle through early \nnext week (esp. for the Charleston Harbor).\n\nCharleston Harbor: With the ongoing environmental conditions, tidal \nlevels could reach 7.5 ft MLLW in the Charleston Harbor during this \nmorning's high tide (~ 9 AM). Thus, plan to issue a Coastal Flood \nAdvisory with this forecast package, which will be in effect from 7 \nAM until 11 AM. Thereafter, it's likely to see minor coastal \nflooding continue through early next week for the Charleston Harbor.\n\nFort Pulaski: Total water forecasts indicate this morning's high \ntide could peak around 9.5 ft MLLW, which would yield minor coastal \nflooding impacts across lower South Carolina and southeast Georgia. \nDespite the borderline nature, have opted to also issue a Coastal \nFlood Advisory for this area, which will be in effect during the \nsame time as listed above. \n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2: Rising temperatures through Tuesday, remaining above \nnormal next week. \n\nToday will feature weak high pressure over the region as the \nnor'easter continues to trek towards New England. Quiet weather is \nexpected today, with clear skies and temperatures right around \nnormal. Clear skies and calm winds tonight will set the stage again \nfor radiational cooling, with lows forecast to drop into the low 50s \ninland with low to mid 60s along the coastline. \n\nAs the nor'easter continues to push further away from the region \nearly this week, high pressure will build into the region. A warming \ntrend is forecast as ridging aloft centered over the Deep South and \nAtlantic high pressure build into the region. By the end of the week \ntemperatures will be back to above normal with highs in the mid to \nupper 80s and some 90s south of I-16 in Georgia. The forecast will \nremain rain free through most of the week. The next real shot at \nrainfall will be late this week into next weekend.",
